  worsened, 117 unchanged, mean +0.0385 (95% CI +0.0149..+0.0622), so the artefact is real and
  significant -- and 0.2211 vs 0.5148 is the same verdict as 0.1825 vs 0.5148.
  THE FLOORS DO NOT MOVE TOGETHER, which is the actual result. population floor 0.5148 -> 0.8881
  (102 of 135 discriminating entries have a population floor of EXACTLY 1.0: the gold constraints
  do not change WHICH rows are selected, only which are FLAGGED -- the testset is compliance-
  shaped). violation floor is 0.0000 BY CONSTRUCTION, because a no-op baseline strips constraints
  and so flags nobody; its skill is the raw F1 in disguise and must not be quoted as skill.
  kind_projected floor is UNCHANGED, because the baseline binds gold's variables.
  So only strict vs kind_projected is a valid comparison. `--variants` prints skill_n per row and
  flags any row whose denominator differs from strict; that column caught a wrong reading of this
  same table within one paragraph of producing it.
  Knock-ons: notes 25.4 gains a mechanism (under kind_projected the S0->S1 gap almost vanishes,
  0.2292 vs 0.2247 -- the skeleton makes the model bind MORE variables, which the strict key
  punishes categorically), and notes 25.5's sufficiency bound rises from 0.3855/0.3614/0.3353 to
  0.4053/0.4211/0.4099 and STILL sits below 0.5148. Quote ~0.41 as the relaxed upper estimate
  beside ~0.35. The "necessary and not sufficient" claim survives with a narrower margin.
  Not built, and worth one line if a reviewer asks: a real floor for the violation half would be a
  FLAG-EVERYTHING query rather than the no-op one.

  (original entry, kept for the pre-registration:)
  `result_set_f1_strict` keys each root binding as (event_indices, object_indices, violated) and
  intersects those tuples, so a prediction that binds ONE EXTRA VARIABLE changes every key and
  scores exactly 0. Measured on the selfcons store, fine-tuned arm, discriminating stratum: 152
  samples (~18% of executed discriminating samples) where gold binds objects only and the
  prediction binds objects AND events have mean objects-only F1 0.5203 and mean `combined` F1
  0.0000 -- and only 4 of the 152 actually select the wrong objects.
  Implemented: `SIMILARITY_VARIANTS` in src/eval_testset.py, one projection table that both
  `_compute_similarity` and audit_testset_discriminability.py iterate, so a variant and its floor
  come from one definition. `strict` (unchanged, primary) / `population` (drop the violation flag)
  / `violation` (flagged situations only, None where gold flags nothing) / `kind_projected` (blank
  the entity kinds gold never binds; diagnostic only, never primary).
  Prediction-side per-case means, fine-tuned discriminating: strict 0.1825, population 0.2392,
  violation 0.1371, kind_projected 0.2211. A fifth variant -- making keys invariant to which
  variable slot an entity landed in -- was tested and DROPPED: it scores 0.1825, identical to
  strict, because no gold entry in that stratum binds two objects in one situation.
  THE RELAXATIONS RAISE THE FLOOR TOO, so the decision variable is skill, not raw F1. First two
  audited entries already show it: entry 1's strict floor is 0.7725 and its `population` floor is
  0.9967. Expect skill to be flat and the finding to be one honest paragraph.
  Regression gate that must keep passing: `--recompute` leaves every per-record `combined` F1 and
  every per-record `skill` bit-identical (verified 3380 records, 0 changed), and
  `summary.variants.strict` equals `result_set_f1_strict` in every block.
  Note for whoever reads the denominators: until the per-variant floors are promoted, `violation`
  undefined-counts differ between arms (128 base vs 378 fine-tuned) because a record with no
  similarity falls back to the strict 0-substitution. With floors present, undefinedness becomes a
  property of the ENTRY and the arms agree. That is the intended behaviour, not drift.

