- PARKED (2026-09-02) G2: INTERACTIVE GROUNDING WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS. The agentic version of
  G1 and the same direction as the AGENTIC WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS entry above; keep them
  together if either is built. Tool surface: `count(type)`, `sample(type, n)`,
  `cardinality(event, object)`, `attribute_values(type, attr)`, and the one that matters,
  `try_query(partial)` returning binding count and execution errors. Today's repair pass
  (src/LLM.py, `verify_ocpq_json` and the execution-error retry) repairs SCHEMA VALIDITY and never
  semantics: the model never learns that its query bound 4237 things where gold binds 1645.
  Three things to carry into the design:
    * PROBING IS NEARLY FREE HERE. ~34.5 s log import against ~30 ms evaluation, so a 20-probe
      budget costs under a second once the import is amortised across a session. Most agentic-query
      work pays per probe. That is a defensible design argument and belongs in the paper.
    * ORACLE DISCIPLINE MUST BE ENFORCED IN CODE, the way `oracle_conditions` was for S1/S2.
      Legitimate feedback: result size, execution errors, whether a variable binds. Illegitimate:
      anything derived from gold. And the reward must NOT be result size -- an agent optimising for
      "returns a plausible number of things" drifts toward the trivial floor query itself, which is
      the one query guaranteed to score 0.5148 while expressing nothing.
    * IT NEEDS A DIFFERENT MODEL. The fine-tuned Llama emits one JSON object and cannot hold a
      multi-turn trajectory, so G2 is a separate arm -- frontier model with tools vs fine-tuned
      small model, one shot -- and must be reported as one. That changes the comparison but supplies
      the ceiling the thesis lacks.

- DONE (2026-09-02) THE TRIVIAL FLOOR WAS UNDERSTATED. Full write-up in
  Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 27. The floor must be the best score obtainable WITHOUT
  understanding the question, and we were using one arbitrarily chosen trivial strategy (the no-op
  baseline) rather than the best of them. A FLAG-EVERYTHING query -- same population, every binding
  flagged -- beats it on 54 of 135 discriminating entries.
  THE FLOOR IS 0.7250 (discriminating) / 0.7803 (overall), not 0.5148 / 0.6124. Skill now divides
  by `trivial_floor` = max over degenerate strategies; `baseline_f1` keeps meaning the no-op score
  so older notes stay true statements about that strategy.
  Biggest single correction: `violation` skill goes +0.1360 -> -0.7285. Its no-op floor was 0.0000
  BY CONSTRUCTION (a baseline with the constraints stripped cannot flag anything), so the model
  looked like it beat trivial on the flagging half. It does not. There is no half of this task
  where it beats a trivial strategy.
  Expressibility was VALIDATED, not assumed: the real always-violated query (`NumChilds min=10**9`
  on an empty child) was built and executed on one entry of each of the eleven logs and matched the
  derived situation set 11/11. Only then was the derivation used for all 169.
  Gates that must keep passing: every similarity block bit-identical after the change (3380/3380),
  0 records with IMPROVED skill (123 worse, 2557 unchanged), and `--floors-only` leaves verdicts,
  situation counts and `baseline_f1` identical to eval_runs/_archive_audit_20260902_prefloor/.
  Cheap to extend: `audit_testset_discriminability.py --floors-only` rebuilds every floor from
  eval_runs/_discriminability_store/ in seconds, so adding a third degenerate strategy costs no
  execution. The floor is a lower bound on ITSELF -- best of two known strategies, not best
  possible. Do not call 0.7250 the ceiling of triviality.

