- KEY FINDING (2026-09-11) THE MODEL IS WORSE THAN A CONSTANT AT CHOOSING ARITY.
    constant "always 0 event / 1 object"  107/169 = 63.3%
    fine-tuned model, constrained          74/169 = 43.8%
    fine-tuned model, free                 54/169 = 32.0%
  Gold's root arity is concentrated: (0,1) is 107/169, (1,1) is 36, (1,0) is 13, and three other
  values share the remaining 13. A predictor that ignores the question entirely beats the model by
  20 points. This is what makes the next step cheap.

- NEXT (2026-09-11) MAKE ARITY NON-ORACLE -- turn the ceiling into a system. Predict (n_event_vars,
  n_object_vars) for the root from the question + schema, then force it with the same per-case
  grammar. It is a 6-class problem with a 63.3% majority class, and notes 32.4 shows anything at
  all beats the model's own 43.8%. Rungs: constant-majority predictor (the floor of this idea),
  then a real classifier, both against N2 = 0.3109 as the ceiling. Cheap: no retraining of the
  generator, and the forcing machinery already exists and is tested.

- REOPENED (2026-09-11) G1 (static log grounding), closed in notes 31 on an oracle that no longer
  applies. That oracle put "solve link_mismatch perfectly" at 0.2820 and found it in no clearing
  combination -- but it was computed BEFORE arity was forced. Recomputed on N2:
    no_result alone                0.6517  (was 0.5643)
    no_result + link_mismatch      0.8063  CLEARS the 0.7250 floor
    no_result + pop_exact          0.7350  clears
  link_mismatch is 59.6% of wrong populations at N2 (was 28.6%) and is what grounding targets.
  GENERAL LESSON, worth applying to every closed direction in notes 26.6: AN ORACLE COMPUTED ON ONE
  DISTRIBUTION STOPS BEING VALID ONCE AN INTERVENTION CHANGES THAT DISTRIBUTION. Re-derive the
  failure budget after anything that moves it.

- NOTE (2026-09-11) `type_mismatch` went 0% -> 26.9% of wrong populations when arity was forced.
  notes 28.2 concluded "type selection is solved"; that held only while arity was free to absorb
  the error. Forced to declare exactly N variables, the model sometimes fills them with the wrong
  types. Not a regression -- a previously hidden error becoming visible.

- CLOSED (2026-09-11) ARITY IS CAUSAL BUT NOT PREDICTABLE by this model. Write-up in
  Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 33. The four-rung ablation was built and DELIBERATELY NOT
  RUN: the gate showed its predictor rung would land below the no-forcing rung. ~30 min of GPU
  across three probes instead of ~8 GPU-hours.
    constant "always (0,1)"                       63.3% acc,  0% minority, EV 6.85
    the model while generating (notes 32.4)       43.8% acc
    prompt v1 (evaluation-log vocabulary)         52.7% acc, 37.1% minority, EV 7.58
    prompt v2 (neutral + procedure + counterex.)  37.9% acc, 33.9% minority, EV 5.82
    prompt v3 (neutral, v1's structure)           46.2% acc, 27.4% minority, EV 6.39
  ACCURACY IS THE WRONG STATISTIC and the first gate used it. A minority-arity hit is worth ~2.3x a
  majority one (notes 32.4: +0.146 vs +0.064), so a predictor can be LESS accurate than a constant
  and worth more -- v1 was exactly that. The gate now scores expected value.
  THE ONLY VERSION THAT PASSED DID SO ON CONTAMINATED VOCABULARY. v3 differs from v1 in ONLY the
  example nouns (v1 used orders/customers/deliveries -- order-management.json is an eval log) and
  EV fell 7.58 -> 6.39. Generation is greedy, so that difference is real, not noise. The clean
  prompts do not beat a constant.
  System estimates, joined against notes 32's per-case F1:
    N1 no forcing   0.2162
    constant (0,1)  0.2268 (wrongly-forced=0) .. 0.2557 (wrongly-forced=N1)
    v3 predictor    0.2028 .. 0.2578        <- BELOW no-forcing under the pessimistic reading
    N2 oracle       0.3109
  A predictor worse than a constant does not merely fail to help, it SUBTRACTS: forcing a wrong
  arity makes the correct query unreachable, while free decoding leaves the model its own 43.8%.
  `(1,0)` recall was 0% in all three versions -- it never once identified a question whose answer is
  a set of events.
  TWO HYGIENE RULES now asserted in tests/test_arity_predictor.py, both learned the hard way:
    * the gold arity distribution must never appear in the prompt (it is a testset label statistic;
      an earlier draft of this work proposed putting it in);
    * no counter-examples -- v2's explicit `wrong: n_event_vars=1, n_object_vars=1` took the error
      it named from 28 cases to 46 and tripled abstentions. Showing a wrong answer primes it.
  THREE ITERATIONS IS THREE ATTEMPTS AT FITTING THE TESTSET. The cap was two; the third was
  justified by a confound the analysis itself introduced. Read the numbers as best-of-three on the
  test data, i.e. optimistic. That they still lose to a constant is what makes the conclusion safe.

- USABLE (2026-09-11) A CONSTANT ARITY PREDICTOR is a legitimate system component and the best
  non-oracle one measured: "always (0,1)", estimated 0.2268-0.2557 against 0.2162 for no forcing.
  NOT tuned on the testset -- (0,1) is also the majority class of the synthetic training corpus
  (34.6%), so the choice is justified from training data alone. Not run as its own rung: +0.011 to
  +0.040 on 135 cases with one greedy sample is inside what a single run resolves, and no decision
  turns on the exact value. Run it if a measured non-oracle number is wanted for the write-up.

